# Getting Started with Inex

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # To try a local checkout use {:inex, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")} instead
  {:inex, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## Introduction

The [INE](https://www.ine.es) (*Instituto Nacional de Estadística*) is Spain's National Statistics
Institute. Through its [API JSON](https://www.ine.es/dyngs/DAB/index.htm?cid=1099) you can access
everything published in INEbase: consumer prices (IPC), the labour force survey (EPA), the
quarterly national accounts (GDP), population, tourism, housing prices and much more.

The API is free and does **not** require an API key, so there is nothing to configure. Let's
alias the modules we'll use and attach the built-in logger so that we can see every request made
to the INE:

```elixir
alias Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

require Explorer.DataFrame

Inex.Telemetry.Logger.attach(level: :info)
```

## Exploring a Series

Series are identified by codes like `IPC290750`: the annual change of the national Consumer Price
Index (CPI). You can find these codes in INEbase (click on any series of a table), in the tables of
an operation or by filtering the series of an operation (see the *Exploring INEbase* notebook).

`Inex.Series.get/2` returns the metadata of a series. With `detail: 2` and `friendly: true` the
INE nests the related objects (operation, periodicity, unit...) with readable names:

```elixir
{:ok, series} = Inex.Series.get("IPC290750", detail: 2, friendly: true)

IO.puts("""
Name:          #{series["Nombre"]}
Operation:     #{series["Operacion"]["Nombre"]}
Periodicity:   #{series["Periodicidad"]["Nombre"]}
Unit:          #{series["Unidad"]["Nombre"]}
Last release:  #{series["Publicacion"]["PubFechaAct"]["Nombre"]}
""")
```

## Fetching Observations

`Inex.Series.observations/2` returns the data exactly as the INE sends it. `last: n` returns the
last `n` periods:

```elixir
{:ok, %{"Data" => data}} = Inex.Series.observations("IPC290750", last: 3, friendly: true)

data
```

Note the `"T3_TipoDato"` field: the most recent value can be a flash estimate (`"Avance"`) that
will be revised in the final release (`"Definitivo"`).

Most of the time you'll want the data in an `Explorer.DataFrame`.
`Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!/2` returns a `date` column plus one column per series:

```elixir
cpi = Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!("IPC290750", observation_start: ~D[2002-01-01])
```

Which we can plot with VegaLite:

```elixir
Vl.new(width: 700, height: 300, title: "CPI - annual change (%)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(cpi)
|> Vl.mark(:area, line: true, opacity: 0.3)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "date", type: :temporal, title: nil)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "IPC290750", type: :quantitative, title: "%")
```

## Dates and Ranges

`observation_start` and `observation_end` take `Date` structs. With `dates` you can request
several dates or ranges at once, for example the CPI of every January since 2020:

```elixir
januaries = for year <- 2020..2026, do: Date.new!(year, 1, 1)

Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!("IPC290750", dates: januaries)
```

```elixir
Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!("IPC290750",
  observation_start: ~D[2022-01-01],
  observation_end: ~D[2022-12-31]
)
```

## Several Series at Once

You can also pass a list of series: they are fetched concurrently and joined by date. Here we compare the CPI annual change (monthly) with the
unemployment rate from the labour force survey (EPA) and the GDP annual growth from the
quarterly national accounts (CNTR), both quarterly:

```elixir
indicators =
  Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!(["IPC290750", "EPA452434", "CNTR6654"],
    observation_start: ~D[2008-01-01],
    rename: %{"IPC290750" => "cpi", "EPA452434" => "unemployment", "CNTR6654" => "gdp"}
  )
```

Monthly and quarterly series share the `date` column, so quarterly series have `nil` values for the
second and third month of every quarter.

For charts with several series the **long** format is more convenient: one row per observation,
with the code, name, unit... of each series:

```elixir
long =
  Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!(["IPC290750", "EPA452434", "CNTR6654"],
    observation_start: ~D[2008-01-01],
    format: :long
  )

Vl.new(width: 700, height: 300, title: "Spanish economy (%)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(DF.select(long, ["date", "name", "value"]))
|> Vl.mark(:line)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "date", type: :temporal, title: nil)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "value", type: :quantitative, title: "%")
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "name", type: :nominal, legend: [orient: :bottom, label_limit: 600])
```

## Metadata Columns

With `metadata: true` the long data frame gets one extra column per variable that defines each
series. This is especially useful for tables (see the next notebooks):

```elixir
Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!(["IPC290750", "IPC290751"],
  last: 3,
  format: :long,
  metadata: true
)
```

## English Responses

Names are in Spanish by default. Use `language: :en` (or `config :inex, language: :en`) to get
them in English:

```elixir
Inex.Series.observations_as_data_frame!("IPC290750", last: 3, format: :long, language: :en)
|> DF.select(["name", "date", "value", "data_type", "unit"])
```

## Errors and Discontinued Series

Every function returns `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, %Inex.Error{}}`. The INE answers unknown
identifiers with an empty response, which Inex reports as `:not_found`:

```elixir
Inex.Series.get("NOT_A_SERIES")
```

Invalid options are caught before making any request:

```elixir
Inex.Series.observations("IPC290750", last: 0)
```

Beware of **discontinued series**: when the INE changes the base or methodology of an
operation, it creates new series codes and stops updating the old ones, which keep answering
with their historical data without any warning. `IPC251852`, the CPI index used in many
examples of the INE documentation, stopped being updated with the 2026 change of the CPI
classification (ECOICOP v2). Always check the date of the latest observation:

```elixir
for code <- ["IPC251852", "IPC290751"] do
  {:ok, %{"Nombre" => name, "Data" => [last]}} =
    Inex.Series.observations(code, last: 1, friendly: true)

  %{code: code, name: name, last_period: "#{last["Anyo"]} #{last["T3_Periodo"]}"}
end
```

Head to the *Exploring INEbase* notebook to learn how to find the series and tables you need.
